# Handbook §26 — access control, not housekeeping.
#
# CORRECTION, 2026-08-11. §26's model is that the repository IS the web root: committing is
# publishing. That stopped being literally true here when the repository was made public so that
# GitHub Pages could serve it. `scripts/stage-site.mjs` now builds the artifact by rule, and only
# site-shaped content reaches the domain — so `brief.md` is readable on GitHub but is NOT served
# from the practitioner's own domain, which is the difference between a colleague finding it and a
# patient finding it.
#
# What has not changed: anything site-shaped — markup, styles, scripts, ANYTHING under `assets` —
# is published the moment it is committed. For those, every path below is still access control.
#
# Deleting a file later does not unpublish it: it stays in edge caches and in search indexes, and
# where the repository is readable it stays in history. The remediation for an accidental commit is
# rewriting history and rotating whatever was exposed — not a deletion commit.

# Internal brand and design material — never published
Branding/
*.ai
*.psd
*.sketch
*.fig

# Working versions and client material
drafts/
client/
*.docx
*.xlsx

# Build output. `_site` is the staged artifact that actually gets published; committing it would
# publish the whole site a second time, under /_site/. `.lighthouseci` holds full HTML reports of
# every page.
_site/
.lighthouseci/

# Local
node_modules/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.env
.env.*
